> **Farzana's take:** Rose gold is the most requested metal I see right now. But most buyers come in asking for rose gold because it is beautiful — they have not thought about how the metal interacts with the stone. For an asscher center in rose gold, you need to hold color tighter than you would in yellow gold. And you need to be careful with the setting choice. The curved, flowing, romantic settings that look stunning in photos can actually fight the asscher's geometry and make the ring look mismatched in person. The Art Deco settings — the ones with milgrain borders and geometric detail — are the ones that make rose gold asscher rings look intentional and complete.

